Kept the recursion out of this version. Start with the sum of the empty subset, which is `0`. For every value, copy the sums already made and add the value to each copied sum.

Dry run for `[2, 5]`:

- Start: `[0]`
- Add `2`: `[0, 2]`
- Add `5`: `[0, 2, 5, 7]`

The `oldSize` variable is important. It makes the loop use only the sums from before the current number. Otherwise, the same number could be used more than once in one pass.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> subsetSums(int[] nums) {
        List<Integer> sums = new ArrayList<>();
        sums.add(0);

        for (int value : nums) {
            int oldSize = sums.size();
            for (int i = 0; i < oldSize; i++) {
                sums.add(sums.get(i) + value);
            }
        }

        return sums;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(2^n)`, because one new sum is made for every existing sum at each step.

Space complexity: `O(2^n)` for the result list. The input array stays unchanged.

A bitmask gives every number two states. If bit `i` is set, `nums[i]` belongs to the subset. Otherwise, it does not.

The mask `0` creates the empty subset, so its sum is correctly added as `0`. This also works when the input is empty.

```cpp
class Solution {
  public:
    vector<int> subsetSums(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(nums.size());
        int totalMasks = 1 << n;
        vector<int> result;
        result.reserve(totalMasks);

        for (int mask = 0; mask < totalMasks; ++mask) {
            int sum = 0;
            for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
                if (mask & (1 << i)) {
                    sum += nums[i];
                }
            }
            result.push_back(sum);
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n * 2^n)` since every mask checks up to `n` positions.

Space complexity: `O(2^n)` for the returned sums. The input vector is not changed.

The helper keeps one running total instead of building the actual subsets. At each index it first skips the number, then tries the branch that includes it.

An input such as `[0]` still gives two answers, `0` and `0`, because the empty subset and the subset containing zero are different subsets. The code keeps both results.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> subsetSums(int[] nums) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        collect(nums, 0, 0, result);
        return result;
    }

    private void collect(int[] nums, int index, int currentSum,
                         List<Integer> result) {
        if (index == nums.length) {
            result.add(currentSum);
            return;
        }

        collect(nums, index + 1, currentSum, result);
        collect(nums, index + 1, currentSum + nums[index], result);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(2^n)`, with one leaf for every possible subset.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the deepest recursive calls, excluding the output list of `2^n` sums.

